0f86336c06067d08552cd170a5e73ea58f213ed18acc987b5f15f1db56","SHADOWING":"881c358f2cf8d26e404e68c14372fd331e626b4d20ad004b7ed015c0ad429ee2","TRANSLATION":"6050a37a10192a29d530623929cb3ad05ec2dab958be815910a43eec77b05c5e"},"sourceWords":[],"translation":"Tạm biệt.","contentStale":true,"manualFields":[],"timingSource":null,"coarseStartMs":431465,"manualLocales":{},"manualSources":{},"thoughtGroups":[],"cefrSuggestion":"A2","ipaNeedsReview":false,"contentWarnings":[],"ipaReviewIssues":[],"dictationEligible":true,"dictationPriority":null,"dictationSelected":true,"ipaDisplayVersion":"learner-ipa-v2","shadowingEligible":true,"shadowingPriority":null,"shadowingSelected":true,"contentTypeSuggestion":"MONOLOGUE"}],"revision":90,"video_id":"Z6pHAPhiQqM","lesson_id":"b42b20ce-1864-4111-9cc5-49da2bef34d3","tts_voice":null,"published_at":"2026-11-22T17:01:54.1098+00:00","published_by":null,"active_job_id":null,"auto_continue":false,"source_end_ms":437000,"timing_status":"VALID","audio_source_id":null,"content_profile":{"key":"4b93ff12f58a560fe790867bccb489c0d00abec7156d192f0b5e0a6106728158","cefr":"B1","manual":false,"contentType":"CONVERSATION"},"source_start_ms":0,"timing_evidence":{"text":" Hi Georgie, how are you? I''m very well, thank you, Neil. I''ve just spent the weekend in the countryside, so I''m feeling very relaxed. I had a lot of fresh air. How are you doing? I''m OK. I''m a bit jealous because the countryside is a fantastic place to relax, and that''s what we''re talking about in this episode of Real Easy English. But people need to know they can get a worksheet and a transcript from our website, bbclearningenglish.com. So, we have talked about the countryside on this podcast in Real Easy English before, but we''ve had some people commenting, asking what actually is the countryside. So, Neil, maybe you can help us out. Well, that''s a good question, actually, because the countryside is the green area outside of the city with hills and grass and fields, but we also talk about villages and small towns outside of the city as the countryside. So you said, my parents live in the countryside. It doesn''t mean they live in a field. No, they live in a small town, but you can say I live in the countryside if you live in a small town. Neil, what do you like about the British countryside? Well, the countryside, like you said, is very relaxing. It''s not busy, and there''s fresh air and you can walk in hills, fields, with great views very easily. What I really like about the British countryside in particular is that it very green and lush That because we get a lot of rain but I also really like being close to the wildlife as well I once came very close to a baby badger which is an animal that you can find in the well it''s actually not very easy to find in the UK. It''s black and white and I saw it during Covid and it came very close to us. Now, you also like leaves, don''t you? Yes, I have a little booklet which has pictures of leaves so that I can identify different trees. So I can... sometimes I go for a walk and I put the pictures up next to the trees and I can identify which ones I''m seeing. And I''ve learnt a lot, actually. Wow. I must come on a walk with you in the countryside. You must. It does slow you down a bit, but we don''t need to have a fast pace when we''re in the countryside. Now, Georgie, what is it about the countryside that you don''t like? A reason I like the countryside is because it''s green, because we get lots of rain, but the downside of all the rain is that sometimes the countryside can be very muddy. So this means that the ground is very wet and sludgy, and it can make your shoes very dirty. So that''s not very fun. What about you? Well, when I was a teenager, I lived in the countryside. And I think it''s kind of boring. If you start liking music and you want to go to concerts and do things which are usually in the city then living in the countryside can be a bit dull Yes, particularly if you live in a place that''s very remote. So a remote place is somewhere that''s far away from towns and cities. It can be very difficult to find things to do or exciting things to do. Yeah, it''s difficult to get around as well unless you drive. So again, unless when you''re a teenager, you have parents who want to drive you around everywhere. That''s very true, actually. When I turned 17, which is the age that you can start driving in the UK, my parents pushed me to take lessons immediately because they were very tired of driving me around everywhere. Now, Neil, I know that you have recently been on a little trip, haven''t you? You went to the Lake District in the north of England. I did, and it''s fantastically beautiful. There are mountains and lakes, and it''s very lush green because of all the rain. And you can go hiking, and you can see fantastic scenery. It''s a great place. Would you say it''s your favourite countryside area in the UK? Yeah, probably. I mean, parts of Scotland are really spectacular, with even bigger mountains. But in England, the Lake District is fantastic. And in Wales, I recently went to the Pembrokeshire coast that in the west of Wales and there just stunning scenery there beaches coastal walks fantastic Yes I saw the photos of that trip It looked amazing My favourite countryside area is probably Cornwall and Devon the southwest of England Once you start driving down there, the roads are very small. There''s not a lot of busy traffic. It can be quite stressful though because the roads are one way and on either side of the road you have these big hedges so you can''t really see around the corners and if a car is coming towards you it''s not good. No, it''s a nightmare. You might have to reverse for ages. Yeah. I really hate that. now then to recap the vocabulary that we''ve used in this episode, starting with countryside. Now, the countryside is the area outside of the cities where not many people live and there''s lots of nature and wildlife. Yes, and wildlife is animals which live in natural environments, for example, badgers and birds. We''ve also heard the adjective lush which describes a green area full of healthy plants and trees. We also heard the adjective remote which describes a place that''s far away from towns and cities. We also heard scenery and scenery is what you can see in a place and in the countryside the scenery is often beautiful. OK, well that is it for this episode of Real Easy English but don''t forget to sign up to our email newsletter. You can get weekly tips and updates from your favourite presenters. We''ll be back next week with another episode, but bye for now. Goodbye.","segments":[{"text":" Hi Georgie, how are you?","end_ms":1520,"start_ms":0},{"text":" I''m very well, thank you, Neil. I''ve just spent the weekend in the countryside,","end_ms":6240,"start_ms":1520},{"text":" so I''m feeling very relaxed. I had a lot of fresh air. How are you doing?","end_ms":11280,"start_ms":6240},{"text":" I''m OK. I''m a bit jealous because the countryside is a fantastic place to relax,","end_ms":16960,"start_ms":11280},{"text":" and that''s what we''re talking about in this episode of Real Easy English.","end_ms":21040,"start_ms":16960},{"text":" But people need to know they can get a worksheet and a transcript from our website,","end_ms":27120,"start_ms":21040},{"text":" bbclearningenglish.com.","end_ms":29040,"start_ms":27120},{"text":" So, we have talked about the countryside on this podcast in Real Easy English before,","end_ms":40560,"start_ms":30000},{"text":" but we''ve had some people commenting, asking what actually is the countryside. So, Neil,","end_ms":46720,"start_ms":40560},{"text":" maybe you can help us out.","end_ms":48400,"start_ms":46720},{"text":" Well, that''s a good question, actually, because the countryside is the green area outside","end_ms":56020,"start_ms":48400},{"text":" of the city with hills and grass and fields, but we also talk about villages and small","end_ms":63960,"